0794 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186350267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350794 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "31c07cc4-fe01-568c-bd6f-14cc9f4cf015" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186348986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350895 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6f29377f-f8a7-5e8e-ae20-7f215e0b9245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186349568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186350112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186350898 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6fdf6e49-b247-581f-8fa7-0908217951a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351291 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351291 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81329" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351291 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351291 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed by the lines $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ $y=4x+6,$ and the curve $y=2{x}^{2}.$ What is ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}{y+1}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351291 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a35004a4-4a15-5b30-b655-d534e6912590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351291 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/5d/5d56dfdb75870a251f1eb94f0305a083489ce9e8dc4b9843138cd8ef88da30ab.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4x+6\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}{y+1}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{4x+6}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}{y+1}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351294 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351294 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81353" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351294 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351294 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{3x}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351294 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2de4beff-bfff-5979-9819-12dd21169f8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351294 #edb/ref 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-x}^{3x}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{-x}^{3x}\\sqrt{x+y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}(x+y)^{3/2}]}_{-x}^{3x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{3}(x+3x)^{3/2}-\\frac{2}{3}(x-x)^{3/2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{3}(4x)^{3/2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{16}{3}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{16}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3/2}\\,dx & =\\frac{16}{3}{[\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}(\\frac{2}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351296 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351296 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351296 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351296 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the double integral\n${∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines\n$x=e,$ $y=e$ and the curve $y={e}^{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351296 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8f9d362-d296-527d-b3eb-8711496d6537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351296 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/aa/aa635ca5ef3198fea56d684b034de476a72e4ace2a4c51b37c6c59977036eb76.png)\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- The line $y=e$ intercepts the curve $y={e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$1\\le x\\le e.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/b7/b78021a06c9fea100b75c66a619c425345cda3666d84da018895be15a53af06d.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dA={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]}_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351370 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351370 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351370 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351370 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6a/6accbfcccdf910acca3aab9f1a12d0ab35c4da683fe78d6ca2d7ada88f28767c.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dA,$\nwhere $D$ is the finite region above the $x$-axis enclosed by the curve $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and the line $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351370 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7ea77d5-2143-55d3-8743-b4a1643dda58" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351370 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that $D$ is a type I region.\n\n- We already know the intersection of the curve with the line $y=0.$\nSo, the $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/db/db0dd47348fb2f9a97c9e0514f74adae05830de7e03f1aea2886eaa95701200e.png)\n\nAs a result, the integral is defined over the following type I region: \n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}[y]_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2}-0)\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380978 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186380979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380978 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380978 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380978 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7cc0afa-bfd9-518f-a964-b4090dd6a25d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380978 #edb/ref 1074 "The integral is defined over the type I region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le x\\}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{x}x\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{x}x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{x}x\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{x}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x{[\\,y\\,]}_{{x}^{3}}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(x-{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{4})dx & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380979 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380981 #edb/ref 1759218604