92186445824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132244" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445824 #edb/ref 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44763ea7-5c70-59d7-96b0-261e9cae9fe6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445824 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/51/51d1e26c53fd0b3ae404df652eed41cd19604d0358ecb5f4e09727580fc57304.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ x & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{x})^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4x \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/05/05740cccba899218f50fc2d9867b968c2b32ca7eaf3ab22a3519387d5756b394.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{2}x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{x/2}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{x/2}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445825 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}4x\\,dy\\,dx=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5a3b41b5-2d15-5da0-8405-0eae05fe096e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445825 #edb/ref 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}4x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x[\\,y\\,]_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4x(x+2-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(4{x}^{2}+8x-4{x}^{3})\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(4{x}^{2}+8x-4{x}^{3})\\,dx & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}+4{x}^{2}-{x}^{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{3}+16-16)-(\\frac{4}{3}+4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}-\\frac{13}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445826 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445826 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445826 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445826 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/45/45675650564d0e0afa5a1cf1a3f4c786c3443374dee2662e9eb44c10f94089c3.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=4x$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65ffb8b6-5f3a-587e-8cef-ea786c6bad11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445826 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $x=-2$ since $x\\ge 0$ in our region. Therefore, $x$-limits are $0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/a7/a7bda76c722de10b988f108c79334227ba2cda82535764ffef76450edaccbb07.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 4x\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{4x}(x+{y}^{2})\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445827 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445827 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81347" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445827 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445827 #edb/ref 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445827 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dc3d2ae-4ebe-55e5-a13e-304e391443f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445827 #edb/ref 1074 "The integral is defined over a type I region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡y}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡0}{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\,dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81322" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445828 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/16/16ec458c15c9427d3d57c6628438b6aa93960778a46c5df162de5d79b87704c9.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0966490-d3a0-58c5-8173-708ac9df5606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445828 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/8f/8f18afd79cafdcd0696b3bc25c05fec4109e54cccb084eef49544a4d6599526c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445829 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186445818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445829 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132116" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445829 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445829 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e7/e72e6dea415bf05a89518d14dcf932d22cc5774919250638716274d71563c621.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445829 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caddd05b-4c41-5309-addf-23d1dae6b262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186445829 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/1a/1ab9ae94853a0d161c74aa8d79c47a38f0b0453e0ece320c367098794d5bcb59.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/8}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/8}^{\\sqrt{x}}f(x,y)\\,dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
